From d9107400d64abd40cd5db615baf290bbe1b5ef57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 13:07:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/cix: don't reprogram DP audio channel status on a live
 stream

hdmi-codec calls audio_startup() before hw_params(), so by the time
dptx_audio_hw_params() runs, SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE is already 1 and the sink is
receiving audio. dptx_audio_setup() then rewrites the channel status --
sampling frequency, word length, channel count -- underneath that live
stream, and the audio InfoFrame is re-sent immediately after. For a short
window the sink is being fed samples whose channel status does not describe
them.

That window is audible, and it is hit far more often than "on format change"
suggests. The HDMI sink node is created by ncz-sky1-audio-autoswitch through
pactl load-module module-alsa-sink, which inherits PipeWire's default
node.suspend-on-idle=true, and WirePlumber suspends an idle node after 5
seconds. Measured on O6N:

    node.name            = "ncz_sky1_hdmi_0_1"
    api.alsa.path        = "hw:0,1"
    node.suspend-on-idle = "true"
    session.suspend-timeout-seconds = 5

So every few seconds of silence closes the PCM, and the next sound reopens
it: startup -> enable -> hw_params -> reconfigure-while-live. That is why the
glitch is reported at the START of playback and at a YouTube advert/content
boundary rather than only when the sample rate genuinely changes -- with
PipeWire pinned to 48000 (allowed-rates is commented out) the hardware rate
does not even change across that boundary; the device merely closes and
reopens.

Mute the SDP audio stream across the reprogramming and restore it only if it
was running, so the sink never sees channel status that disagrees with the
samples. Reconfiguring a stopped stream is unchanged.

Also fix TIMESTAMP_INTERVAL, the one register in dptx_audio_setup() written
without the per-source offset. Inert today because every caller passes
source 0, but it would corrupt SEC1 the moment a second audio source is used.

Not fixed here, and worth doing separately: hw_params() returns 0 early when
trilin_dp_plugged_status() is false, which silently skips rate programming
altogether if a plug-status blip coincides with the reconfigure, leaving the
previous stream's channel status in place.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/cix/dptx/trilin_dptx_audio.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/cix/dptx/trilin_dptx_audio.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/cix/dptx/trilin_dptx_audio.c
index 98f6c758f0cb..275c580e9d45 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/cix/dptx/trilin_dptx_audio.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/cix/dptx/trilin_dptx_audio.c
@@ -72,7 +72,8 @@ static void dptx_audio_setup(struct trilin_dp *dp, int source, int freq,
 	/* copyright*/
 	trilin_dp_write(dp, TRILIN_DPTX_SEC0_CS_COPYRIGHT + offset,
 			DPTX_CS_COPYRIGHT);
-	trilin_dp_write(dp, TRILIN_DPTX_SEC0_TIMESTAMP_INTERVAL,
+	/* Every other register here is offset by source; this one was not. */
+	trilin_dp_write(dp, TRILIN_DPTX_SEC0_TIMESTAMP_INTERVAL + offset,
 			DPTX_CS_TIMESTAMP_INTERVAL);
 	trilin_dp_write(dp, TRILIN_DPTX_SEC0_AUDIO_CHANNEL_MAP + offset,
 			DPTX_CS_AUDIO_CHANNEL_MAP_DEFAULT);
@@ -159,10 +160,32 @@ static int dptx_audio_hw_params(struct device *dev, void *data,
 		params->sample_width, params->channels,
 		params->cea.channel_allocation);
 
+	/*
+	 * hdmi-codec calls audio_startup() BEFORE hw_params(), so by the time we
+	 * get here SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE is already 1 and the sink is receiving an
+	 * audio stream. dptx_audio_setup() then rewrites the channel status
+	 * (sampling frequency, word length, channel count) underneath it, so the
+	 * sink briefly sees a live stream whose channel status does not describe
+	 * it. That is audible as a glitch or a burst of noise every time the PCM
+	 * is (re)configured -- which is at the start of every playback, because
+	 * the HDMI sink node is created with node.suspend-on-idle=true and is
+	 * torn down after a few seconds of silence. A YouTube advert/content
+	 * boundary is exactly that: silence, suspend, resume, reconfigure.
+	 *
+	 * Mute the SDP audio stream across the reprogramming and restore it
+	 * afterwards, so the sink only ever sees channel status that matches the
+	 * samples being sent.
+	 */
+	if (dp_audio->running)
+		trilin_dp_write(dp, TRILIN_DPTX_SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE, 0);
+
 	dptx_audio_setup(dp, 0, params->sample_rate, params->sample_width,
 			 params->channels);
 	dptx_audio_send_infoframe(dp, 0, &params->cea);
 
+	if (dp_audio->running)
+		trilin_dp_write(dp, TRILIN_DPTX_SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE, 1);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
-- 
